1. What is HQL?

HQL (Hibernate Query Language) is the query language provided by the Hibernate framework. Instead of working directly with database tables and columns, HQL works with Java entity classes and their properties.

This means that developers write queries using Java class names and field names rather than database table names and column names.

Example

Suppose we have an entity class:

@Entity
public class Employee {
    private int id;
    private String name;
    private double salary;
}

An HQL query would be:

FROM Employee

Here, Employee is the Java entity class, not the database table.

2. What is JPQL?

JPQL (Java Persistence Query Language) is the standard query language defined by the Java Persistence API (JPA).

Like HQL, JPQL also works with entity classes and their attributes instead of database tables.

Since JPQL is part of the JPA specification, it is supported by all JPA providers such as:

Hibernate
EclipseLink
OpenJPA
3. HQL and JPQL are Object-Oriented Query Languages

Unlike SQL, HQL and JPQL do not directly access database tables.

Instead, they work with:

Java Entity Classes
Entity Objects
Entity Attributes
Relationships between entities
SQL Example
SELECT * FROM employee;

Here employee is a database table.

HQL / JPQL Example
FROM Employee

Here Employee is a Java Entity Class.

4. JPQL is a Subset of HQL

JPQL contains only the standard features defined by JPA.

Hibernate provides some additional features beyond the JPA specification.

Therefore,

Every JPQL query is a valid HQL query.
Not every HQL query is a valid JPQL query.

This is why JPQL is called a subset of HQL.

5. Supported Operations

Both HQL and JPQL support the following operations:

SELECT

Used to retrieve records.

Example

SELECT e FROM Employee e
UPDATE

Used to update existing records.

Example

UPDATE Employee e
SET e.salary = 50000
WHERE e.id = 1
DELETE

Used to delete records.

Example

DELETE FROM Employee e
WHERE e.id = 1
6. HQL Supports INSERT

One important difference is that HQL supports the INSERT statement, whereas JPQL does not.

Example

INSERT INTO Employee(name, salary)
SELECT e.name, e.salary
FROM OldEmployee e

This feature is available only in Hibernate.

Difference Between SQL and HQL/JPQL
SQL	HQL / JPQL
Works with database tables	Works with Java entity classes
Uses column names	Uses entity attributes
Database dependent	Object-oriented
Directly communicates with database	Works through Hibernate/JPA
Difference Between HQL and JPQL
HQL	JPQL
Hibernate-specific	JPA standard
Supports additional Hibernate features	Supports only JPA features
Supports INSERT	Does not support INSERT
Every JPQL query works in HQL	Not every HQL query works in JPQL
Advantages of HQL and JPQL
Object-oriented query language.
Queries use Java entity classes instead of database tables.
Reduces dependency on database-specific SQL.
Easier to maintain when entity names remain unchanged.
Supports relationships between entities.
Improves code readability.
Integrates seamlessly with Hibernate and JPA.
Key Points
HQL stands for Hibernate Query Language.
JPQL stands for Java Persistence Query Language.
Both are object-oriented query languages.
They work with Java entity classes instead of database tables.
JPQL is a subset of HQL.
Both support SELECT, UPDATE, and DELETE operations.
Only HQL supports the INSERT statement.
HQL is Hibernate-specific, whereas JPQL is the standard query language defined by JPA.

